//  PETPORTS -- HUE LAMP MODULE LIGHT
//
//  THE COLOUR HERE IS A STARTING VALUE AND NOTHING MORE. The script overwrites
//  it on the first update, which is a twelfth of a second after the effect is
//  applied, so this is only ever seen on the frame between.
//
//  DIM CYAN, BECAUSE THAT IS THE FIRST FRAME THE SCRIPT COMPUTES, AND NEITHER
//  HALF OF THAT IS GUESSABLE.
//
//  CYAN: the script seeds hue 0, and wave(0) is 0 -- so RED is the channel
//  switched off at hue 0. That is the source function's own phase convention,
//  not a porting slip; verified against the original across the whole wheel.
//  The sweep then runs backwards from there by default, blue, magenta, red,
//  yellow, green and round, which is every hue in an order nobody has to like.
//
//  DIM: 80 rather than 255, because `intensity` in the .statuseffect scales
//  every channel and 80 is what it ships at. THIS NUMBER TRACKS THAT ONE. Retune
//  the intensity and this seed is stale -- worth one frame at a twelfth of a
//  second, which is the whole exposure, so it is not worth a mechanism.
//
//  Seeding this to match the first computed frame is what keeps the handover
//  invisible; seeding it grey like the other two lamps would put one frame of
//  grey in front of every socket.
//
//  POSITION IS [0, 0] BECAUSE THAT IS THE BODY CENTRE, which is what lets the
//  flip script the vanilla augment needs go away. Every unit chassis in this
//  mod has its collisionPoly centred on the origin, so a centred light looks
//  identical mirrored.
//
//  THE LIGHT'S NAME IS WHAT THE SCRIPT ADDRESSES. animator.setLightColor takes
//  it, so it is spelled once here and once in the script and nowhere else.

{
	"lights" : {
		"petports_module_huelamp" : {
			"active" : true,
			"position" : [ 0.0, 0.0 ],
			"color" : [ 0, 80, 80 ],
			"pointLight" : true
		}
	}
}
